#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1200.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222357} :activity/id #uuid "d3cfa4d0-9bee-52e4-bb9e-39444a417cfd" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050615 :topic/id #uuid "58ea9fa1-e1b7-56f7-a58d-173ed320b2af" :topic/math-academy-id 6369} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222357 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222476 :tutorial/content "In a previous lesson, we learned how to compute Laplace transforms. We now examine how the Laplace transform changes when a function is multiplied by an exponential.\n\nSuppose\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)\n$$\n\nexists for $s>s_0.$ We want to compute the Laplace transform of $e^{at}f(t).$\n\nStarting from the definition of the Laplace transform,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,{e}^{at}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s-a)t}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow recall that\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\int_0^{\\infty} e^{-st}\\,f(t)\\,\\textrm{d}t,\n\\qquad s>s_0.\n$$\n\nComparing the two integrals, we see that\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\infty} e^{-(s-a)t}\\,f(t)\\,\\textrm{d}t\n=F(s-a).\n$$\n\nFor this to make sense, we need $s-a>s_0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\ns>s_0+a.\n$$\n\nThis result is known as the **First Shifting Theorem**:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{at}f(t)\\} = F(s-a).\n$$\n\nIn the next slide, we will apply this result to a concrete example." :tutorial/id #uuid "44078a68-8d27-5f6e-adec-348668ecef51" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14326 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "23a4eff8-b2bf-51f0-87ea-b1466d7af3ca" :step/math-academy-id 600094 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222358}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222358 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222509 :tutorial/content "The **First shifting Theorem** states that if\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)\n$$\n\nfor $s>s_0$, then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{e^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a),\n\\qquad s>s_0+a.\n$$\n\nSo, when you multiply a function by $e^{at}$, you do two things:\n\n- *Replace* $s$ with $s-a$ in the known transform $F(s)$.\n\n- *Shift the domain* from $s>s_0$ to $s>s_0+a$.\n\nTo see this in action, consider the following example.\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{\\sin(\\omega t)\\}=\\frac{\\omega}{s^2+\\omega^2},\n\\qquad s>0.\n$$\n\nSuppose we want to compute\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{2t}\\sin(3t)\\}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we identify the base transform:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{\\sin(3t)\\}=\\frac{3}{s^2+3^2}\n$$\n\nNext, we apply the first shifting theorem with $a=2$, which means we replace $s$ by $s-2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\} & =\\frac{3}{(s-2)^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{(s-2)^{2}+9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the original transform is valid for $s>0$, the shifted transform is valid for\n\n$$\ns>2.\n$$\n\nThis example illustrates the general pattern: multiplying by $e^{at}$ leads to a simple substitution $s:= s-a$, along with a corresponding shift in the domain." :tutorial/id #uuid "511f0e8e-f19d-5e1e-955e-97dbd8386ea2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14590 :tutorial/title "Using the First Shifting Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "3390cec0-8a87-5c98-86f2-ba843c736c0d" :step/math-academy-id 600095 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222359}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222359 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221433 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221155 :question/id #uuid "fe6b394f-a130-5746-a5c9-0fbbee020554" :question/math-academy-id "e-21424"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a921795-a527-5528-afcb-5881f188a665" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048865}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221927} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221946} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304947} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304948} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304949} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304950} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441314} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441320}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the First Shifting Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "fc54e4ac-72ee-57e2-8339-74eb6e586087" :step/math-academy-id 600096 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222360}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222360 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221434 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221167 :question/id #uuid "05394517-ee39-5b77-9f84-b439d907dd3e" :question/math-academy-id "e-21590"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1dd5c036-7a05-5ca4-aae9-1cf585839ebb" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049301}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222042} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222044} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304984} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304995} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304997} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305000} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368088} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368095} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441315} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441318} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445925} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498560}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the First Shifting Theorem and Linearity"} :step/id #uuid "58c13b52-ac11-56f0-9289-23be6f240ad2" :step/math-academy-id 600100 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222361}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222361 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222508 :tutorial/content "Recall that the first shifting theorem states that if\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{f(t)\\right\\} = F(s),\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{e^{at}f(t)\\right\\}  = F(s-a).\n$$\n\nThis means the new Laplace transform is the original graph of $F$ evaluated at $s-a$.\n\nSo, the graph shift is:\n\n- If $a>0$, then $F(s-a)$ is the graph of $F(s)$ **shifted to the right** by $a$ units.\n\n- If $a<0$, then $F(s-a)$ is the graph of $F(s)$ **shifted to the left** by $|a|$ units.\n\nFor example, suppose $F(s)$ has the following graph:\n\n![](images/45/45ce62c981e4c7853ef158046eb21fbc9c135f5fa0a8887d813a07594077c8ce.png)\n\nThen the Laplace transform of $e^{-3t} f(t)$ is $F(s+3).$ Here, $a=-3$, so this corresponds to shifting the graph of $F(s)$ three units to the left:\n\n![](images/b7/b75658824079fe90c9c4f43d4d37d355c95b845ebed3f11bdf751b52f8d34f55.png)\n\nA useful self-check is that any horizontal features move the same way:\n\n- vertical asymptotes shift left/right by the same amount,\n\n- intercepts and key points shift left/right by the same amount." :tutorial/id #uuid "db14b380-2e8e-5007-a9ad-722a83b574ae" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14589 :tutorial/title "Graph Shifting"} :step/id #uuid "c7227d83-b5a7-5418-8e1d-915b257ae283" :step/math-academy-id 600103 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222362}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222362 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221435 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221162 :question/id #uuid "1e5e6e3a-306b-5e8c-a8ba-baa0d63a1813" :question/math-academy-id "e-21549"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b6ba61d-2e00-539a-84d0-84b63bf92be0" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048419}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221930} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221947} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304979} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304980} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304983} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368081} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391762} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402100} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445926} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498513} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535049}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Graph Shifts From the First Shifting Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "b89a14ea-72ce-5ea2-90f1-e3c54fe52d78" :step/math-academy-id 600104 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222363}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222363 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221436 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221159 :question/id #uuid "04233a8a-069f-5649-b198-c6bbf5b96742" :question/math-academy-id "e-21531"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aeb2589b-0135-5bbb-bc91-ec121ee5defd" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048865}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221931} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221945} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304944} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304945} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304946} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368062} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390920} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396318} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441316} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441317} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441319} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441321} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535087}] :knowledge-point/title "Proving the First Shifting Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "3f432000-4480-52b3-b24b-72186edeb9f6" :step/math-academy-id 600108 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222364}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222364 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222520 :tutorial/content "Using the first shifting theorem, we can obtain new Laplace transforms from old ones without performing any additional integration.\n\nIf\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)\n$$\n\nfor $s>s_0$, then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{e^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a),\n\\qquad s>s_0+a.\n$$\n\nThe table below shows some general results we can obtain using the first shifting theorem.\n\n| $f(t)$ | $F(s)=\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{f(t)\\right\\}$ | Compare With |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $e^{at} t^n$ | $\\dfrac{n!}{(s-a)^{n+1}}$ | $\\mathcal L \\{t^n\\} = \\dfrac{n!}{s^{n+1}}$ |\n| $e^{at}\\sin \\omega t$ | $\\dfrac{\\omega}{(s-a)^2+\\omega^2}$ | $\\mathcal L\\{\\sin\\omega t\\} = \\dfrac{\\omega}{s^2+\\omega^2}$ |\n| $e^{at}\\cos \\omega t$ | $\\dfrac{s-a}{(s-a)^2+\\omega^2}$ | $\\mathcal L\\{\\cos\\omega t\\} = \\dfrac{s}{s^2+\\omega^2}$ |\n\nSo, each new entry in our table is obtained by taking a known transform and replacing $s$ with $s-a$, and then shifting the domain accordingly." :tutorial/id #uuid "dbf3c06b-f0f4-528b-aff6-5b6adcae65b8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14692 :tutorial/title "A Laplace Transforms Comparison Table"} :step/id #uuid "aa6dc187-37cc-58ad-bd19-cba1f304979e" :step/math-academy-id 600111}] :activity/title "The First Shifting Theorem of Laplace Transforms" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186221462}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481743} :activity/id #uuid "4e0c95fe-b957-5da5-82b5-741cf5414583" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050615 :topic/id #uuid "58ea9fa1-e1b7-56f7-a58d-173ed320b2af" :topic/math-academy-id 6369} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481696 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445926 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07208325-b6fe-56ff-ab78-18466949f3ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The Laplace transform is unchanged." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445913} {:answer/id #uuid "7dcba25b-c390-5edc-b740-7305051f595a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Scale the graph of $F(s)$ by a scale factor of $7$ vertically." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445915} {:answer/id #uuid "9b26e88d-f446-59c8-b01e-ca9d9e592906" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Scale the graph of $F(s)$ by a scale factor of $7$ horizontally." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445917} {:answer/id #uuid "c94dc5ec-cf48-51ff-9ce3-01c9fcc65d9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $7$ units to the left." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445919} {:answer/id #uuid "ff77f603-3667-5d57-9f2f-4b85ac75f514" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $7$ units to the right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445922}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445919} :answer-field/id #uuid "0524c331-6564-52ef-aba2-9b3e8fe558a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445923}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b5d66a4d-16c1-5cfd-be68-7c518337e0a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-332062" :question/problem "Consider a function $f(t)$ for which the Laplace transform is $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s).$ How can we obtain the graph of $L\\{{e}^{-7t}f(t)\\}$ from the graph of $F(s)?$" :question/worked-solution "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$\n\nThis implies that multiplying a function $f(t)$ by ${e}^{at}$ shifts the corresponding Laplace transform horizontally along the $s$-axis.\n\n- If $a>0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the right.\n\n- If $a<0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the left.\n\nIn our case, to get the graph of $L\\{{e}^{-7t}f(t)\\},$ we must shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $7$ units to the left."} :step/id #uuid "4de91bb7-3162-59ca-b8a7-380041a9f451" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481742}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481699 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445925 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5812230e-36ca-591f-b89d-48a8b7e1dcc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{s+3}+\\frac{7}{(s-5)^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445914} {:answer/id #uuid "83050161-c9bb-5cd0-b5d2-7a04f0e38764" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{s-3}+\\frac{14}{(s-5)^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445916} {:answer/id #uuid "a6286f17-855d-5d36-bc99-ac311ecdbde2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{s+3}+\\frac{14}{(s-5)^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445918} {:answer/id #uuid "eb1cbeb1-e8a7-518d-a6fb-cbc0586e7fc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{s+3}+\\frac{14}{(s+5)^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445920} {:answer/id #uuid "f81a2466-a55c-5f7e-b7c2-eccdac0d7ed6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{s-3}+\\frac{14}{(s+5)^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445921}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445918} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbbbb7ac-d5cd-51d2-b97c-84f0232078de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445924}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "34e7ccd4-a685-5844-a89f-5c57b735778f" :question/math-academy-id "q-332187" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nuse linearity of Laplace transforms and the first shifting theorem to find the Laplace transform of  $f(t)=2{e}^{-3t}+7{t}^{2}{e}^{5t}$ for $s>5.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the linearity properties of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{f(t)\\} & =L\\{2{e}^{-3t}+7{t}^{2}{e}^{5t}\\} \\\\  & =L\\{2{e}^{-3t}\\}+L\\{7{t}^{2}{e}^{5t}\\} \\\\  & =2L\\{{e}^{-3t}\\}+7L\\{{t}^{2}{e}^{5t}\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that, for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{2}\\} & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first shifting theorem states that if $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$ then $L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{2}{e}^{5t}\\} & =\\frac{2}{(s-5)^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{f(t)\\} & =2L\\{{e}^{-3t}\\}+7L\\{{t}^{2}{e}^{5t}\\} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+3}+7\\cdot \\frac{2}{(s-5)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{s+3}+\\frac{14}{(s-5)^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis transform is valid for $s>5.$"} :step/id #uuid "58a277dc-7f40-5be3-81ea-a2342d28f19a"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481735 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304946 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16979a0a-5fab-5b5c-86c2-4f69494ac5bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{-9t}d(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302602} {:answer/id #uuid "2a895488-234e-5a29-b81f-90f3846bbf81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{st}\\cdot {e}^{9t}d(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302726} {:answer/id #uuid "8b629cb7-7b7c-531d-8fad-665f318f2f54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{st}\\cdot {e}^{-9t}d(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303323} {:answer/id #uuid "f4153c16-35ab-527c-b3df-82aae0d04617" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{9t}d(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303987}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302602} :answer-field/id #uuid "507ebdd5-031a-5857-a408-9e2e5d22e0e9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304296} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12f90244-ded0-5104-9e2a-acaa009da0ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{(s-9)t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302582} {:answer/id #uuid "9621b9e5-3ffe-54bf-853e-df74494cbdb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{(s+9)t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303390} {:answer/id #uuid "aec08836-db99-58c9-a73d-f62929c8f01e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-(s+9)t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303532} {:answer/id #uuid "cc6df75b-e60b-5730-9a9d-90666210572f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-(s-9)t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303716}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303532} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c34bde0-d16f-513e-8175-6d2d86285a07" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304368} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0eea1ccd-542e-57f0-9eb9-ed18e4ca2120" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "D(s-9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302554} {:answer/id #uuid "8105c990-fc3a-59dc-b3e6-f0c23dcaee70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "D(s+9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303260} {:answer/id #uuid "ff8385d6-891f-59da-bcc0-9395986fb6da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "D(s)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304067}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303260} :answer-field/id #uuid "9551a75c-29a8-543d-ab36-f0e92966feb6" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304475} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d813493-43b7-5abc-9c6e-eebce057cbdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s>{s}_{0}-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302546} {:answer/id #uuid "85c306d3-e46b-5ed9-a2ef-51b1eeedd1a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s>{s}_{0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303287} {:answer/id #uuid "dabedf8c-de8d-51ba-aae6-ea177e4aa201" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s>{s}_{0}+9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303816}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302546} :answer-field/id #uuid "c66ea00f-ab02-54a7-8591-f30db0c5b86b" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304618}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d4d9c5d8-fd8f-53ad-985b-edcda3948d5a" :question/math-academy-id "q-331808" :question/problem "Consider a function $d(t)$ such that its Laplace transform is $L\\{d(t)\\}=D(s)$ for $s>{s}_{0}.$ Suppose we wish to find $L\\{{e}^{-9t}d(t)\\}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nBy definition of the Laplace transform, we can write \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{-9t}d(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-1}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-2}}\\,d(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={{field-3}}\\,\\text{for}\\,{{field-4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Starting from the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{-9t}d(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{-9t}\\,d(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s+9)t}\\,d(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $D(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,d(t)\\,\\text{d}t,$ where $s>{s}_{0}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s+9)t}\\,d(t)\\,\\text{d}t=D(s+9),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\ns+9>{s}_{0}\\,⇒\\,s>{s}_{0}-9.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d471051a-4a59-5227-9393-1b386051ee7b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481696}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481742 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402100 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dbeddd1b-c3dd-54cd-a00c-bfa7f8bb9029" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Scale the graph of $F(s)$ by a scale factor of $9$ horizontally." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402102} {:answer/id #uuid "20dad633-ddf9-5439-b4ea-d46bf03e30dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $9$ units to the right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402103} {:answer/id #uuid "502d4257-08f1-572c-b4f7-44ca7819cd6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $9$ units to the left." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402104} {:answer/id #uuid "6d2964df-d6ec-5d47-adc1-edddefca6e19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The Laplace transform is unchanged." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402105} {:answer/id #uuid "e92b73b6-6083-5681-b869-7b2bec74f5ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Scale the graph of $F(s)$ by a scale factor of $9$ vertically." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402106}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402104} :answer-field/id #uuid "8decbbab-7268-5304-9585-788574a6793b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402101}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f8dd5186-15e7-5cde-a17c-1b124d1cef2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-332064" :question/problem "Consider a function $f(t)$ for which the Laplace transform is $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s).$ How can we obtain the graph of $L\\{{e}^{-9t}f(t)\\}$ from the graph of $F(s)?$" :question/worked-solution "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$\n\nThis implies that multiplying a function $f(t)$ by ${e}^{at}$ shifts the corresponding Laplace transform horizontally along the $s$-axis.\n\n- If $a>0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the right.\n\n- If $a<0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the left.\n\nIn our case, to get the graph of $L\\{{e}^{-9t}f(t)\\},$ we must shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $9$ units to the left."} :step/id #uuid "e19c971d-0087-5320-ade5-7561c8259640" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481699}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481743 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222044 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4aca0833-9adf-5c73-a1ab-d07568183f99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{(s+2)^{2}}+\\frac{2}{s-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355332} {:answer/id #uuid "8290279a-5ab8-57ac-9792-73f13640f201" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{(s+2)^{2}}+\\frac{1}{s-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355812} {:answer/id #uuid "8f0198a4-4fab-5362-bb17-7ad99d588499" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{(s+2)(s-2)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355926} {:answer/id #uuid "91c23727-f24d-5c74-bf75-ea326ecfa263" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{(s+2)^{2}(s-2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355953} {:answer/id #uuid "d05463d4-fa4b-567c-9712-d946fd6321f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s+2}+\\frac{2}{(s-2)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356485}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356485} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e1a231c-22ee-57a9-969d-ae5213d3dae3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356969}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "94d7e94a-1d17-5470-a293-1353d69fb49f" :question/math-academy-id "q-89592" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nuse linearity of Laplace transforms and the first shifting theorem to find the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{-2t}+2t{e}^{2t}$ for $s>2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the linearity properties of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{f(t)\\} & =L\\{{e}^{-2t}+2t{e}^{2t}\\} \\\\  & =L\\{{e}^{-2t}\\}+L\\{2t{e}^{2t}\\} \\\\  & =L\\{{e}^{-2t}\\}+2L\\{t{e}^{2t}\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that, for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{t\\} & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first shifting theorem states that if $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$ then $L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{t{e}^{2t}\\} & =\\frac{1}{(s-2)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{f(t)\\} & =L\\{{e}^{-2t}\\}+2L\\{t{e}^{2t}\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{(s-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+2}+\\frac{2}{(s-2)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis transform is valid for $s>2.$"} :step/id #uuid "eb252da1-42be-5946-a2d3-d509b332da4e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481735}}] :activity/title "The First Shifting Theorem of Laplace Transforms" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481570}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498616} :activity/id #uuid "d391d32b-b16d-540c-996a-eea9e96f6f32" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050615 :topic/id #uuid "58ea9fa1-e1b7-56f7-a58d-173ed320b2af" :topic/math-academy-id 6369} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498613 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498560 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c0f16cb-5174-5b73-b5f7-d769c600bca2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{24}{(s-2)^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497973} {:answer/id #uuid "6b5ee011-1e91-5398-889f-6887925f5543" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{6}{(s-2)^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498061} {:answer/id #uuid "de1efeda-62c3-5e17-b7cb-b295d6451ef1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{4}{(s-2)^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498295} {:answer/id #uuid "f22261e4-628e-5669-8943-4998a47f8540" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{12}{(s-2)^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498331} {:answer/id #uuid "ff0b72cf-c5ca-5854-b15b-0478c4cfc654" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{24}{(s-2)^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498359}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498359} :answer-field/id #uuid "d796c28f-05c4-5d13-97d9-d47a85425db2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498469}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8828e2f6-2765-54b6-be68-2960b77fe33e" :question/math-academy-id "q-332127" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nuse linearity of Laplace transforms and the first shifting theorem to find the Laplace transform of $f(t)=6t+4{t}^{3}{e}^{2t}$ for $s>2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the linearity properties of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{f(t)\\} & =L\\{6t+4{t}^{3}{e}^{2t}\\} \\\\  & =L\\{6t\\}+L\\{4{t}^{3}{e}^{2t}\\} \\\\  & =6L\\{t\\}+4L\\{{t}^{3}{e}^{2t}\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that, for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{t\\} & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}} \\\\ L\\{{t}^{3}\\} & =\\frac{6}{{s}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first shifting theorem states that if $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$ then $L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nL\\{{t}^{3}{e}^{2t}\\}=\\frac{6}{(s-2)^{4}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{f(t)\\} & =6L\\{t\\}+4L\\{{t}^{3}{e}^{2t}\\} \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}+4\\cdot \\frac{6}{(s-2)^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{24}{(s-2)^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis transform is valid for $s>2.$"} :step/id #uuid "1ca3e022-cc39-56cd-87c5-f311545581d9"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498616 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221931 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f08c7bbb-c2d0-5cc0-bc38-5213764f4024" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{-2t}f(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390812} {:answer/id #uuid "3d94bd8e-1095-5dca-a3f8-9aaf610978e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{2}f(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390813} {:answer/id #uuid "28bb7e5b-3455-5522-9000-aba6a7f1eb41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{2t}f(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390814} {:answer/id #uuid "3a6f9bc4-f8e2-5df6-ba18-334846863668" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{st}\\cdot {e}^{2t}f(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390815}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390814} :answer-field/id #uuid "70e2f98a-16ab-5ff0-97ea-4bd74b0c49aa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390811} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65e2db0c-613e-5799-bbf1-d1fd16890715" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(s)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390817} {:answer/id #uuid "0de35569-0f52-5e9a-9e72-6a51d6ee0586" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(s-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390818} {:answer/id #uuid "4389475f-b93b-5c1b-b101-b0afcf3c492c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(s+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390819}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390818} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6bfa42b-b843-5d7d-9658-950091ed7693" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390816} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "918e65b1-37a4-57c9-91f8-31e6cedfd3d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{(s+2)t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390821} {:answer/id #uuid "0bc84124-f50e-5d24-b3dd-36e70598d6cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{(s-2)t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390822} {:answer/id #uuid "563f5f3b-f94c-5c9e-9225-6b76c3213e92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-(s+2)t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390823} {:answer/id #uuid "8caaf550-00be-527a-9b74-16e9740ed790" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-(s-2)t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390824}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390824} :answer-field/id #uuid "20869d57-2780-5f3e-8816-87767b181302" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390820} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fcb831e6-ab7e-56db-8853-cf8c7142bb92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s>{s}_{0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390826} {:answer/id #uuid "9a50ffe3-b486-5fce-9a67-a9c4583d6c5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s>{s}_{0}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390827} {:answer/id #uuid "3ef90db9-1b5a-5b95-9214-0ce85e84b3f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s>{s}_{0}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390828}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390827} :answer-field/id #uuid "88721178-9ecd-58e0-9a04-bd9972d88e44" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390825}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0c13572d-a427-531e-bc5a-c3129ec5167c" :question/math-academy-id "q-328705" :question/problem "Consider a function $f(t)$ such that its Laplace transform is $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)$ for $s>{s}_{0}.$ Suppose we wish to find $L\\{{e}^{2t}f(t)\\}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nBy definition of the Laplace transform, we can write \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{2t}f(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-1}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-2}}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={{field-3}}\\,\\text{for}\\,{{field-4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Starting from the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{2t}f(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{2t}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s-2)t}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t,$ where $s>{s}_{0}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s-2)t}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=F(s-2),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\ns-2>{s}_{0}\\,⇒\\,s>{s}_{0}+2.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2727354f-cd77-55e1-a1d0-cd9c6788d154" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498639}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498627 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498513 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17ddf7a6-02de-5357-a0f7-fda2afff17f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/97/97b2ac07998dea2ba0116ae4b1b26bccdc2c6b2a64d99836578c01d5502c61d2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497855} {:answer/id #uuid "385d8719-8052-5ba5-93c6-443dbaca8ec8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/60/6095f8d90e29e62054e66ce058aaf9ef8b592b61a991e7ce9d93b6759d330c7f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497923} {:answer/id #uuid "38be91b4-49a4-5c7a-9985-97b2fa85e3e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/fb/fb0bda88019114bf77d68c8c72d164bfa21abc1c10cf0fe52d72b53c1ddbe102.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497924} {:answer/id #uuid "7370344f-b222-597a-9d15-849b3ab68fb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/ee/ee965e81bc2b44c9d00c08dcd4f227b76c927fb467921e2fac664834b6bb978a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498074} {:answer/id #uuid "9339d5e1-6245-5bf5-bba6-2750f42458db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/ba/ba6fb6239cc146b1fb5fc31300015984826e4ab1fe4061a0bd14ce3b2643569f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498134}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497855} :answer-field/id #uuid "64110326-a77e-5f76-b890-3c31d6f5dae0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498404}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2ff7cb38-62cd-5c8f-a2e0-afb3c4fd3adf" :question/math-academy-id "q-332120" :question/problem "![](images/ba/ba6fb6239cc146b1fb5fc31300015984826e4ab1fe4061a0bd14ce3b2643569f.png)\n\nConsider a function $f(t)$ for which the graph of the Laplace transform $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $L\\{{e}^{t}f(t)\\}?$" :question/worked-solution "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$\n\nThis implies that multiplying a function $f(t)$ by ${e}^{at}$ shifts the corresponding Laplace transform horizontally along the $s$-axis.\n\n- If $a>0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the right.\n\n- If $a<0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the left.\n\nIn our case, to get the graph of $L\\{{e}^{t}f(t)\\},$ we must shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $1$ unit to the right.\n\n![](images/97/97b2ac07998dea2ba0116ae4b1b26bccdc2c6b2a64d99836578c01d5502c61d2.png)"} :step/id #uuid "657640d3-5628-5d33-809c-aa5fdbf0b2e4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498613}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498639 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304980 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14a729c4-4d54-527d-a93f-3a54c3407e68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Scale the graph of $F(s)$ by a scale factor of $8$ horizontally." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302590} {:answer/id #uuid "18dea247-7ffa-5cb9-8234-c442fc60d964" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Scale the graph of $F(s)$ by a scale factor of $8$ vertically." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302621} {:answer/id #uuid "21cdcb93-5dad-510a-96e8-42da2d959713" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $8$ units to the right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302668} {:answer/id #uuid "34be62be-1361-5365-8bef-9d8bbbb458d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $8$ units to the left." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302784} {:answer/id #uuid "84d48414-295f-59b5-ab18-a8e9f863808e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The Laplace transform is unchanged." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303283}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302668} :answer-field/id #uuid "44c6a275-2026-5967-a17a-985587e234ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304266}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f3bbef6f-6296-5a54-8079-8df45d564e16" :question/math-academy-id "q-332063" :question/problem "Consider a function $f(t)$ for which the Laplace transform is $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s).$ How can we obtain the graph of $L\\{{e}^{8t}f(t)\\}$ from the graph of $F(s)?$" :question/worked-solution "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$\n\nThis implies that multiplying a function $f(t)$ by ${e}^{at}$ shifts the corresponding Laplace transform horizontally along the $s$-axis.\n\n- If $a>0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the right.\n\n- If $a<0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the left.\n\nIn our case, to get the graph of $L\\{{e}^{8t}f(t)\\},$ we must shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $8$ units to the right."} :step/id #uuid "9646d192-bbba-563e-9982-b0be272fde36" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498627}}] :activity/title "The First Shifting Theorem of Laplace Transforms" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497796}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535130} :activity/id #uuid "f0cfce06-cdd0-55ee-a1fd-d19a40d86dbe" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050615 :topic/id #uuid "58ea9fa1-e1b7-56f7-a58d-173ed320b2af" :topic/math-academy-id 6369} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535120 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535049 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b2047a0-77e5-59eb-b2d8-c4830bf40585" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $6$ units to the left." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534323} {:answer/id #uuid "5c3fa65d-99cc-5c77-bd71-1f41ffaf620f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Scale the graph of $F(s)$ by a scale factor of $6$ horizontally." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534451} {:answer/id #uuid "ab5d9892-a999-54fb-b2bc-6e3cfce7f088" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $6$ units to the right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534652} {:answer/id #uuid "b0c4c853-8f85-580a-b3fc-4912e7fb4cda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Scale the graph of $F(s)$ by a scale factor of $6$ vertically." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534659} {:answer/id #uuid "f45c8098-fbed-5d8d-a23a-ce5636def254" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The Laplace transform is unchanged." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534810}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534652} :answer-field/id #uuid "07472d6e-7c20-5717-a8e5-c9592eadbbb0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534831}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a295f504-6868-5fd6-8219-b8135f071992" :question/math-academy-id "q-332061" :question/problem "Consider a function $f(t)$ for which the Laplace transform is $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s).$ How can we obtain the graph of $L\\{{e}^{6t}f(t)\\}$ from the graph of $F(s)?$" :question/worked-solution "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$\n\nThis implies that multiplying a function $f(t)$ by ${e}^{at}$ shifts the corresponding Laplace transform horizontally along the $s$-axis.\n\n- If $a>0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the right.\n\n- If $a<0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the left.\n\nIn our case, to get the graph of $L\\{{e}^{6t}f(t)\\},$ we must shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $6$ units to the right."} :step/id #uuid "a11ab2b3-b4ab-5878-9174-f53555687bd5"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535122 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441316 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a509f0a1-df96-54e7-8b07-3244182c4bb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{st}\\cdot {e}^{5t}z(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441269} {:answer/id #uuid "b90a7aa8-ab0c-5cbf-81a4-db4e580b95d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{-5t}z(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441276} {:answer/id #uuid "c0a3a294-7cc6-5b9f-b486-dfeccdcf0123" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{5t}z(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441278} {:answer/id #uuid "fbb5df36-957b-5e85-9043-a3a5a566794f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{st}\\cdot {e}^{-5t}z(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441293}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441276} :answer-field/id #uuid "258bced0-5662-54ae-9abc-a8aa92bf71d0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441298} {: